## 2026-09-13 (fifty-seventh session) — no version bump — `DEBT-004` relocation done properly; a bug caught and fixed along the way; tracker triage

**Note on session numbering**: this session started from the same base commit (`66c6273`) as the fifty-fifth/fifty-sixth sessions above, without visibility into each other — both independently self-numbered "fifty-fifth" at first. Renumbered to fifty-seventh (after, not instead of, their fifty-sixth) when reconciling the two pushes; the migration file below is numbered `017` for the same reason (`015`/`016` were already claimed by their session by the time this one\'s work got pushed).

**Trigger**: continuing the standing session workflow after Mahdi confirmed he\'d completed the `dev-export` one-time setup (`dev_export_secret` in the live `config.php`, `DEV_EXPORT_SECRET` GitHub Actions secret). `git pull` on session start showed nothing new, and `docs/TRACKER_SNAPSHOT.md` still didn\'t exist — checked the workflow\'s run history via the GitHub API directly (`GET .../actions/workflows/dev-export-snapshot.yml/runs`) and found **zero runs ever**, not a failure, just the 6-hour schedule not having fired yet. Manually dispatched it (`POST .../dispatches`) to test the full pipeline immediately rather than waiting — run `34714140136` completed with `conclusion: success` within about 15 seconds. `git pull` afterward brought in `docs/TRACKER_SNAPSHOT.json`/`.md` for the first time, containing real production data.

**This mattered beyond just testing infrastructure**: reading that file answered Mahdi\'s own multi-session-old question about whether his live edits actually worked. They did — `BUG-051`, `BUG-052`, and `DEBT-001` all show `closed` in the real tracker (he tested and closed them himself), and there are zero open bugs left. Two annotations were sitting untriaged: `ANN-006` (verbatim the same complaint already fully captured in `DEBT-002`\'s own technical description — a duplicate never marked closed when that triage happened) and `ANN-008` (genuinely new: "for each annotation you show the x and y position ... it would also be good if you give more details regarding the element the user pointed at like its name its container or technical designation").

### `DEBT-004` — the tests/ relocation half, done properly

Before touching anything: grepped `REPOSITORY_ARCHITECTURE.md` and found its own status note explicitly says the tests/ relocation was **evaluated and deliberately deferred** by the **ninth session** — "relative-`require` coupling made this the lower-risk call per the spec\'s own \'where practical\' wording." `DEBT-004` itself (opened sessions thirty-six through thirty-nine, then carried untouched across many more) never cross-referenced that decision anywhere in its own tracker text — it was re-flagged as plain oversight when it had actually been a considered, reasoned call. Worth naming plainly: this is exactly the kind of cross-session context loss the tracker system and now `docs/TRACKER_SNAPSHOT.md` exist to prevent, and it happened anyway, in this same project, on this exact item.

Investigated the *actual* coupling before deciding whether to proceed: `grep`\'d for `require`/`__DIR__` across both test files. Only `smoke_test.php` has any relative-path coupling at all — 9 lines, all pointing into `src/backend/{data,engine}/`. `http_api_test.php` is a pure HTTP client with zero filesystem coupling. This is meaningfully narrower than the ninth session\'s context (a simultaneous, much larger multi-tree architecture migration), which is presumably why "lower-risk" made sense there but doesn\'t necessarily still apply to this one narrow, standalone move today.

**What shipped**: `git mv src/backend/tests tests/backend` (history preserved). `smoke_test.php`\'s 9 requires fixed. `Makefile` updated (`test`, `test-http`, `build-deploy` targets — the two `cd src/backend && ...` invocations simplified to plain repo-root-relative calls, since neither test file needs a specific working directory once paths are fixed). `.github/workflows/build-test-publish.yml` updated (3 steps — 2 test-invocation steps had their now-unnecessary `working-directory: src/backend` removed entirely, 1 artifact-assembly `cp` line updated). `check-repo-hygiene.sh`\'s stale-pre-restructure-path pattern extended to include the old `src/backend/tests` path, so any future reintroduction of it gets caught automatically — required adding three narrow, well-commented exceptions for genuinely historical text (two migrations\' own stored tracker-item prose, plus the auto-generated `docs/TRACKER_SNAPSHOT.json`, which will self-resolve on its next refresh).

### A real bug, introduced and caught within this same session

The first fix gave `smoke_test.php` a single hardcoded relative path (`__DIR__ . \'/../../src/backend/data/...\'`) — correct for running the file from its new repo location, but **wrong** for the *other* real place this exact file lives: copied verbatim into `_deploy/tests/` by the same `build-deploy` Makefile target, unchanged by this session. In the deploy artifact, `data/`/`engine/` are flat siblings of `tests/` one level up, not two levels up through a `src/backend/` that doesn\'t exist there at all.

`make build-deploy` → `check-deploy-artifact.sh` reported a clean pass — **a false pass**. Traced why: the checker\'s require-resolution logic (`realpath -m` then `[ -f "$resolved" ]`) only checks that *something* exists at the resolved path, never that the resolved path stays *inside* the artifact directory. Run from inside this full repo checkout, `_deploy/tests/../../src/backend/data/parameters.php` resolves by climbing straight back out of `_deploy/` into the real `src/backend/` sitting right next to it — which is a real file, so the check passed, while telling us nothing about whether a genuinely isolated deployment would work.

Confirmed the gap concretely: copied `_deploy/` to `/tmp/isolated_deploy_test/` (nothing else in that directory at all) and ran `php tests/smoke_test.php` from there directly — `PHP Fatal error: ... Failed opening required \'/tmp/isolated_deploy_test/tests/../../src/backend/data/parameters.php\'`. Exactly the failure mode a real production deployment would hit.

**Fixed properly**: `smoke_test.php` now resolves `$backendRoot` at runtime — `is_dir(__DIR__ . \'/../../src/backend\') ? __DIR__ . \'/../../src/backend\' : __DIR__ . \'/..\'` — then requires everything relative to that. Verified in both directions by the same true-isolation method: repo context (`php tests/backend/smoke_test.php` from the repo root, 24/24) and a fresh isolated copy of the rebuilt artifact (24/24) — not just trusting the automated checker, which had just been shown to have a blind spot for exactly this.

### `DEBT-006` (new, found and fixed same session): hardened `check-deploy-artifact.sh` itself

Logged the checker\'s own gap as its own tracker item rather than silently patching around it. Fix: after `realpath -m` resolves a required path, also assert it starts with the artifact directory\'s own `realpath` — anything that resolves to a real file **outside** the artifact now fails loudly (`ESCAPING_REQUIRES`), distinct from the existing "resolves to nothing" (`MISSING_REQUIRES`) case. Verified two ways: the real, now-fixed artifact still passes cleanly (positive test), and a scratch copy with the exact original broken pattern reintroduced correctly fails (negative test) — confirming the hardened check actually catches this class of bug rather than just happening to pass by coincidence again.

Also documented, in the checker\'s own comments, a real remaining limitation rather than leaving it silent: `smoke_test.php`\'s requires now go through a runtime `$backendRoot` variable, not a literal `__DIR__ . \'...\'` string, so the checker\'s regex-based scan (which specifically greps for that literal pattern) doesn\'t see them at all — not a false pass, just a blind spot for this one deliberately-dynamic file. Judged not worth engineering the static check to understand PHP runtime branches for one file; the true-isolation manual test already performed is a *stronger* verification for this specific file than the automated check provides for anything else anyway (it actually executes the code, rather than only proving a path resolves to some file).

