### Two rounds of test-assertion fallout, both legitimate, neither a real regression

1. Adding `DEBT-006`/`FEAT-013` via migration pushed seeded `tracker_items` from 66 to 68 — the same two hard-coded count assertions from the previous session\'s own churn (64→66) needed the same treatment again (66→68). Comment strings updated to explain the running total\'s provenance rather than just the bare number.
2. `FEAT-012`\'s own `comments` field (written this session) legitimately mentions "`BUG-051`/`052`/`DEBT-001` closed" in prose — which a case-insensitive substring search for `BUG-051` correctly also matches now, alongside `BUG-051` itself. The old test asserted *exactly* one result; loosened to "at least one result, `BUG-051` included" instead, since asserting an exact global count against a tracker whose whole design encourages cross-referencing text between items was always going to keep breaking as that text accumulates — this isn\'t a one-off fix so much as a more correctly-scoped assertion going forward.

### A third issue, worth its own note: this session\'s migration first draft looked like a SQL bug but wasn\'t

First attempt at this session\'s own tracker migration (numbered `015` at the time it was written, before the fifty-fifth/fifty-sixth sessions\' own `015`/`016` were discovered already pushed and this one renumbered to `017` — see the note at the top of this entry) failed with a foreign-key violation on the final `INSERT INTO tracker_updates` statement. Spent real effort ruling out a SQL-escaping bug first — checked every apostrophe in the file for correct `\'\'`-doubling, ran the file directly through the `mysql` CLI (which parses multi-statement files with a real, correct SQL parser, unlike `db/Migrations.php`\'s own deliberately-simplified custom splitter) to get MySQL\'s own precise error location rather than PDO\'s wrapped one, and confirmed the SQL itself was syntactically 100% valid — the CLI reached the very last statement in the file before failing. The actual cause: `ANN-006`/`ANN-008` are **real, production-only rows** (created live by Mahdi in the app, never part of any migration\'s seed data), so they don\'t exist in a fresh local/CI database at all. The `UPDATE ... WHERE code = \'ANN-006\'` statements are correctly no-ops in that case (0 rows affected is not an error) — but a plain `INSERT INTO tracker_updates (item_code, ...) VALUES (\'ANN-006\', ...)` immediately afterward then violates the foreign key, since the parent row genuinely doesn\'t exist in this environment. **Fixed** by switching those six `tracker_updates` inserts from `INSERT ... VALUES` to `INSERT ... SELECT ... WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM tracker_items WHERE code = \'...\')` — safe and correct in both a fresh local/CI database (silently inserts nothing for the two codes that don\'t exist there) and the real production database (inserts normally, since both codes are genuinely present there).

